## 8. Remote Work and Home Office Security

### 8.1 Remote Work Environment

Employees working from home must:
- Use a secure WiFi network (password-protected, not public WiFi)
- Keep the home office area clean and secure (no sensitive documents left visible)
- Use privacy screens when working in shared spaces or near windows
- Close/lock doors when accessing sensitive information
- Not discuss confidential information with family members or others in the home

### 8.2 Public WiFi and Traveling

**Public WiFi Risks:**
- Do NOT access company systems on unsecured public WiFi without VPN
- Do NOT work with customer data on public WiFi
- Always use VPN when working remotely from coffee shops, airports, hotels, etc.

**Traveling:**
- Use hotel WiFi only with VPN enabled
- Do not leave company devices unattended
- Consider using privacy screen when working in public spaces
- Be aware of people looking over your shoulder ("shoulder surfing")

---

## 9. Communication Security

### 9.1 Email Security

**Email Best Practices:**
- Do not email passwords or sensitive credentials
- Use encryption for email with sensitive attachments
- Verify the recipient email address before sending
- Do not click suspicious links or download unexpected attachments

**Phishing Prevention:**
- Be cautious of emails requesting urgent action or claiming account issues
- Verify sender identity (check email address, not just display name)
- Hover over links to see actual URL before clicking
- Report suspicious emails to security@brightpath.com

### 9.2 Instant Messaging (Slack)

**Slack Usage:**
- Use private channels for sensitive discussions, not open channels
- Do not share passwords or credentials in Slack
- Be aware that Slack messages are logged and may be reviewed
- Sensitive file transfers should use encrypted methods, not Slack

**Slack File Sharing:**
- Do not share confidential documents in public channels
- Use private direct messages or private channels for sensitive information
- Be cautious of whom you grant access to file downloads

### 9.3 Video Conferencing

**Zoom, Google Meet, Microsoft Teams:**
- Use password-protected meetings for sensitive discussions
- Do not record meetings without participant consent (and document it in meeting minutes)
- Be aware of who can see your screen and what information is visible
- Close applications with confidential information before screen sharing
- Use "waiting room" to control participant entry
- Disable screen sharing for participants who don't need it

